\details{
The distance between samples \eqn{x} and \eqn{y} is
\deqn{\sqrt{\sum_i \left(x^*_i - y^*_i\right)^2},}
where \eqn{x^*} are the \code{\link[=philr]{philr()}} coordinates of \eqn{x} with part weights
\code{'enorm.x.gm.counts'} and ILR weights \code{'blw.sqrt'} (Silverman et al.
2017, Methods), so that

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{philr_distance(x, tree) ==
  euclidean(philr(x, tree, part_weights = 'enorm.x.gm.counts', ilr_weights = 'blw.sqrt'))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

For other weightings, use \code{euclidean(philr(x, tree, ...))}. With uniform
weights, PhILR distances are Aitchison distances (\code{\link[=aitchison]{aitchison()}}): the
weights are what make them phylogenetic. The part weights are computed
from all the samples, so adding or removing a sample changes the
distances between the others. \code{pairs} limits which distances are
computed, not the samples used.

The tree is handled as \code{\link[=philr]{philr()}} handles it: tips that aren't features
are dropped, and polytomies are resolved, with a warning (see
\strong{The tree} in \code{\link[=philr]{philr()}}). It needs branch lengths.
